          "text": "<p>$$<br>\\begin{gathered}<br>S \\rightarrow a S|b B| \\epsilon \\\\<br>B \\rightarrow b S \\mid a B<br>\\end{gathered}<br>$$</p><p><br>The Myhill-Nerode equivalence classes for the language generated by this grammar are:</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$\\left\\{w \\in(a+b)^* \\mid \\# a(w)\\right.$ is even $\\}$ and $\\left\\{w \\in(a+b)^* \\mid \\# a(w)\\right.$ is odd $\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\left\\{w \\in(a+b)^* \\mid \\# b(w)\\right.$ is even $\\}$ and $\\left\\{w \\in(a+b)^* \\mid \\# b(w)\\right.$ is odd $\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\left\\{w \\in(a+b)^* \\mid \\# a(w)=\\# b(w)\\right\\}$ and $\\left\\{w \\in(a+b)^* \\mid \\# a(w) \\neq \\# b(w)\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\{\\epsilon\\},\\left\\{w a \\mid w \\in(a+b)^*\\right\\},\\left\\{w b \\mid w \\in(a+b)^*\\right\\}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "B",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 121"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "494664",
          "text": "<p>Consider the Pushdown Automaton (PDA) specified below, which accepts by final state.</p><ul><li>States $Q:\\left\\{q_0, q_1, q_2\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>Input $\\Sigma:\\{a, b\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>Stack $\\Gamma:\\left\\{A, Z_0\\right\\}\\left(Z_0\\right.$ is the initial stack symbol)<br>&nbsp;</li><li>Start State: $q_0$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>Final State $F:\\left\\{q_2\\right\\}$</li></ul><p>The state transition function $\\delta$ is given by the following diagram. The notation $x, Y \\rightarrow \\alpha$ means \"on input $x$, pop $Y$, and push $\\alpha$ \".</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><b id=\"docs-internal-guid-b63f7f91-7fff-4f2c-dc95-f2d32e06437a\"><img width=\"550\" height=\"156\" alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></b><p>Which one of the following sequences must follow the string aaab so that the overall string is accepted by the automaton?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$b$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$b a$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$b b$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$b b a$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 121"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "494656",
          "text": "<p>Which one of the following languages over the alphabet $\\{a, b\\}$ is described by the regular expression:</p><p>$$<br>(a+b)^* a(a+b)^* a(a+b)^* ?<br>$$</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>The set of all strings containing the substring $a a$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>The set of all strings containing at most two $a$ 's<br>&nbsp;</li><li>The set of all strings containing at least two $a$ 's<br>&nbsp;</li><li>The set of all strings that start and end with either $a$ or $b$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 121"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "494666",
          "text": "<p>Let $\\langle M\\rangle$ denote the standard encoding of a machine $M$. Consider the following languages.</p><ul><li>$L_1=\\{\\langle A\\rangle \\mid A$ is a DFA and $|L(A)| \\geq 100\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_2=\\{\\langle A, G\\rangle \\mid A$ is a DFA, $G$ is a CFG, and $L(G) \\subseteq L(A)\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_3=\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid M$ is a Turing Machine and $L(M)$ is regular $\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_4=\\left\\{\\langle G\\rangle \\mid G\\right.$ is a CFG and $\\left.L(G)=\\Sigma^*\\right\\}$</li></ul><p>Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$L_1$ IS DECIDABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_2$ IS DECIDABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_3$ IS UNDECIDABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_4$ IS UNDECIDABLE.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "A;B;C;D",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 121"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "494660",
          "text": "<p>Consider the alphabet $\\Sigma=\\{a, b\\}$, the null/empty string $\\epsilon$, and the set of strings $S, A$, and $B$ generated by the corresponding non-terminals of a regular grammar. $S, A$, and $B$ are related as follows:</p><ol><li>$S=a A$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$A=b A+a B+\\epsilon$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$B=a A$</li></ol><p>Which one of the following choices precisely represents the strings in $S$ ?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$a\\left(b^*(a a)^*\\right)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$a(b+a a)^*$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$(a(b+a a))^*$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$a\\left(b^*+(a a)^*\\right)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "B",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 121"
